#[derive(Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum ReplCommand {
    /// A line that is Redblue source rather than a command.
    ///
    /// This is not [`ReplCommand::Unknown`]: `say "hi"` is not a mistyped
    /// command, it is a program. Conflating the two made the REPL answer
    /// `2 + 3` with "Unknown command '2 + 3'" and refuse to evaluate it.
    NotACommand,
    Quit,
    Help,
    Clear,
    History,
    Vars,
    Functions,
    /// Runs a shell command in the host shell and reports its output and exit.
    ///
    /// The command text is kept whole rather than split into words: the shell
    /// is the parser for it, and `!echo a | wc -l` is one command with its own
    /// pipeline in it.
    Shell(String),
    /// Sends the session's last result to a shell command's standard input.
    Pipe(String),
    /// Moves the session's working directory, which every relative file
    /// argument is resolved against.
    ChangeDir(String),
    /// Prints the session's working directory.
    PrintWorkingDirectory,
    /// Lists the session's working directory.
    ListDirectory,
    Load(String),
    Save(String),
    /// Reads a history file written by [`ReplCommand::Save`] back into the
    /// session's history.
    ///
    /// The other half of `:save`, which had no half: a saved file could not be
    /// loaded by any REPL path, so the round trip existed only in unit tests.
    ///
    /// The dot spelling of this same word is the in-memory half: `.restore
    /// <name>` restores a checkpoint this session made, while `:restore <file>`
    /// reads a saved history file. The two never share storage, and the split is
    /// stated once in the help text rather than repeated on every line.
    Restore(String),
    /// Saves the session's bindings, last result and history position under a
    /// name this session can return to.
    ///
    /// A checkpoint is session state and nothing else: it is never a file, never
    /// committed, and dies with the session.
    Checkpoint(String),
    /// Lists the checkpoints this session holds, in creation order, marking the
    /// current one.
    Sessions,
    /// Steps one binding change back, to the state before the last source line
    /// that changed anything.
    Undo,
    /// Steps one undone binding change forward again.
    Redo,
    /// Writes the session — every line it ran, what each line said, and what
    /// failed — as Markdown, to `path` or to stdout when given nothing.
    ///
    /// `Option` for the same reason [`ReplCommand::Prompt`] is: `.export` with no
    /// path is not a command missing its argument, it is a command writing to the
    /// terminal, which is the one destination that needs no name.
    Export(Option<String>),
    Reset,
    Run(String),
    /// Re-runs the file the last `:load` or `:run` named.
    ///
    /// The path was remembered by the command that read it, so a session
    /// iterating on a file does not have to retype the path between runs.
    Reload,
    /// Formats the block this session is holding, or turns formatting every
    /// entered line on or off.
    ///
    /// `Option` because `.format` on its own is a command with a job — format
    /// the buffer — and `.format on|off` is the same command setting a session
    /// preference, rather than a command typed incompletely.
    Format(Option<String>),
    /// Opens the debug sub-REPL on an expression or a file, or — with no
    /// argument — on nothing at all, which is the empty session the sub-REPL
    /// commands are then asked against.
    ///
    /// `String` rather than a unit variant for the same reason [`Run`] is: the
    /// argument is the program that runs on entry, and `.debug 1 + 1` that
    /// ignored its argument printed breakpoint status instead of running it.
    /// Same shape as [`ReplCommand::Run`], which does not ignore it either.
    Debug(String),
    /// Registers an expression to be re-evaluated at every step of a program
    /// running inside the debug sub-REPL, reporting it when it changes.
    Watch(String),
    /// Shows the call stack of the last step taken in the debug sub-REPL,
    /// outermost frame first.
    Stack,
    /// Shows the bindings of the frame the debug sub-REPL last stepped in:
    /// its parameters and its own locals, not its caller's.
    Locals,
    /// Leaves the debug sub-REPL, returning the session to its parent prompt
    /// with the parent's state untouched.
    Back,
    /// Sets a breakpoint on a line, or clears one that is already set there.
    Break(String),
    /// Lists the lines the session has breakpoints on.
    Breaks,
    /// Removes the breakpoint on a line.
    Delete(String),
    /// Arms a step into the next call, for the next program the session runs.
    Step,
    /// Arms a step over the next call, for the next program the session runs.
    Next,
    /// Runs the program to its end, or to the next breakpoint.
    Continue,
    /// Prints the frame the program last stopped in.
    Frame,
    Inspect(String),
    /// Answers "what is this?" — the value, its kind, and where it was written.
    ///
    /// The argument is an *expression*, not a name: `.type [1, 2]` is a question
    /// about a value that no session has ever bound.
    Type(String),
    /// The whole environment the session can reach: its own bindings, the
    /// modules it imported with their members, and the language's builtins.
    Env,
    /// Every distinct record and object shape the session holds, with fields.
    Types,
    /// The fields and methods of one value.
    ///
    /// The argument is an *expression*, like [`ReplCommand::Type`]'s: the
    /// question is about a value, and a value need not be one the session bound.
    Dir(String),
    /// Every session binding with the type it currently holds.
    Typehints,
    /// Prints a value as an indented structure — one level of indentation per
    /// level of nesting.
    ///
    /// The argument is an *expression*, like [`ReplCommand::Type`]'s: what is
    /// being asked about is a value, and a value need not be one the session
    /// ever bound.
    Pretty(String),
    /// Prints a value as JSON.
    ///
    /// The same value the language's own `json.stringify` writes, so what the
    /// REPL shows and what a program can produce are one document.
    Json(String),
    /// Prints a list of records as aligned columns, one header row naming the
    /// keys and one row per record.
    Table(String),
    /// Lists what a module exposes, from the registry rather than from prose.
    Doc(String),
    /// Says where a name comes from: a builtin, this session, a module member, or
    /// nothing at all.
    Which(String),
    /// The shape of a call — its parameters and what it answers.
    Sighelp(String),
    /// Finds a keyword across builtin shapes, the help text, this session's
    /// bindings and its history.
    ///
    /// The argument is a keyword, not a name: `.search split` answers with every
    /// place `split` appears, which is the question `.which`, `.sighelp` and
    /// `:history` each answer only one quarter of.
    Search(String),
    /// Runs an expression N times against the session and reports wall time.
    ///
    /// The argument is the measurement, not a name: `.time 1 + 1` is a question
    /// about code the session never bound.
    Time(String),
    /// The same, reported as a distribution over a hundred runs.
    Bench(String),
    /// What the session is carrying: its bindings, its history, and an estimate
    /// of the heap those bindings hold, each labelled.
    ///
    /// Observing only: no binding is read into a clone, no history entry moves,
    /// and the last result is left exactly as it was.
    Mem,
    /// Where the time went in the last multiline block, one figure per source
    /// line, slowest first.
    ///
    /// A block rather than the last single line, because one line has one figure
    /// and that is what `.time` already answers.
    Profile,
    Ast(String),
    Tokens(String),
    /// The instruction listing an expression compiles to.
    ///
    /// The argument is an expression, as for [`ReplCommand::Ast`]: the question
    /// is about code, and the code need not be one the session has run.
    Bytecode(String),
    /// The same chunk, annotated: constants table and a comment per instruction.
    Asm(String),
    /// Runs a program on both engines and says whether they answered alike.
    ///
    /// The argument is a program, not a name: `.compare 1 + 2` is a question
    /// about code the session has not bound, and `.compare say 1` is a question
    /// about what running it does on each engine.
    Compare(String),
    Example,
    /// The guided tour: a numbered set of steps, each with code to run.
    ///
    /// Separate from [`ReplCommand::Example`] because they answer different
    /// questions — one is "what can I write", the other is "how do I start" — and
    /// a newcomer given the catalogue alone gets a wall rather than a way in.
    Tutorial,
    /// The hint for the tutorial step this session is on.
    ///
    /// A command rather than nothing at all: a reader who has lost the thread of
    /// the tour needs a way back into it that is not retyping it.
    Hint,
    /// Opens `$EDITOR` over the block this session is holding, and runs what
    /// comes back.
    ///
    /// A block can be typed, but a fifty-line one has to be typed: without this
    /// there was no way to *paste* one into a session at all.
    Edit,
